{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03950","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03950","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03950","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shahriar Kabir","familyName":"Nahin","name":"Nahin, Shahriar Kabir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxiao","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Wenxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Joshua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anshuman","familyName":"Chhabra","name":"Chhabra, Anshuman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hongfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Is The Performance Ceiling of My Classifier? Utilizing Category-Wise Influence Functions for Pareto Frontier Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T21:33:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:43:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-29T18:36:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-31T00:05:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data-centric learning seeks to improve model performance from the perspective of data quality, and has been drawing increasing attention in the machine learning community. Among its key tools, influence functions provide a powerful framework to quantify the impact of individual training samples on model predictions, enabling practitioners to identify detrimental samples and retrain models on a cleaner dataset for improved performance. However, most existing work focuses on the question: \"what data benefits the learning model?\" In this paper, we take a step further and investigate a more fundamental question: \"what is the performance ceiling of the learning model?\" Unlike prior studies that primarily measure improvement through overall accuracy, we emphasize category-wise accuracy and aim for Pareto improvements, ensuring that every class benefits, rather than allowing tradeoffs where some classes improve at the expense of others. To address this challenge, we propose category-wise influence functions and introduce an influence vector that quantifies the impact of each training sample across all categories. Leveraging these influence vectors, we develop a principled criterion to determine whether a model can still be improved, and further design a linear programming-based sample reweighting framework to achieve Pareto performance improvements. Through extensive experiments on synthetic datasets, vision, and text benchmarks, we demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach in estimating and achieving a model's performance improvement across multiple categories of interest."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03950","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-31T02:35:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Simple Policy Gradients for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:53:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-01T03:54:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:59:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-23T05:31:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-24T00:25:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion large language models (dLLMs) represent a promising alternative to autoregressive LLMs; however, the lack of effective post-training techniques, including reinforcement learning (RL), remains a key challenge for dLLMs, especially for downstream applications. Existing approaches often rely on a sequence-level view that requires biased likelihood approximations. In this work, we propose Amortized Group Relative Policy Optimization (AGRPO), a policy gradient algorithm that leverages the Markovian nature of dLLMs, optimizing individual denoising steps rather than full sequences. Our approach improves alignment between the trained policy and the inference process and also admits efficient, unbiased gradient updates via a novel timestep estimation scheme. We demonstrate AGRPO's effectiveness on different math and reasoning tasks, achieving absolute accuracy gains of +59.4\\% and +69.7\\% on Countdown and Sudoku over the base LLaDA model, exceeding comparable methods such as diffu-GRPO. Code is available at https://github.com/probablyabot/agrpo."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages. ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:13Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-24T02:11:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03954","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03954","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03954","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Bary","name":"Bary, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiffanie","familyName":"Godelaine","name":"Godelaine, Tiffanie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Axel","familyName":"Abels","name":"Abels, Axel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoît","familyName":"Macq","name":"Macq, Benoît","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimizing Resources for On-the-Fly Label Estimation with Multiple Unknown Medical Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T21:41:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-30T01:09:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/bhi67747.2025.11269527","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate ground truth estimation in medical screening programs often relies on coalitions of experts and peer second opinions. Algorithms that efficiently aggregate noisy annotations can enhance screening workflows, particularly when data arrive continuously and expert proficiency is initially unknown. However, existing algorithms do not meet the requirements for seamless integration into screening pipelines. We therefore propose an adaptive approach for real-time annotation that (I) supports on-the-fly labeling of incoming data, (II) operates without prior knowledge of medical experts or pre-labeled data, and (III) dynamically queries additional experts based on the latent difficulty of each instance. The method incrementally gathers expert opinions until a confidence threshold is met, providing accurate labels with reduced annotation overhead. We evaluate our approach on three multi-annotator classification datasets across different modalities. Results show that our adaptive querying strategy reduces the number of expert queries by up to 50% while achieving accuracy comparable to a non-adaptive baseline. Our code is available at https://github.com/tbary/MEDICS"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables, Accepted at IEEE BHI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03954","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:07:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03896","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03896","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03896","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muzhi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Muzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Canyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Canyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yating","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yating","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GAE: Unleashing Physical Potential of VLM with Generalizable Action Expert"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:33:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-11T03:17:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T00:23:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models demonstrate strong reasoning and planning abilities, yet grounding these predictions into precise robot actions remains a central challenge. Existing Vision-Language-Action methods typically entangle reasoning and action generation, leading to limited generalization. We propose Generalizable Action Expert (GAE), a task-agnostic model that converts sparse geometric plans into dense robot actions. Our approach introduces a sparse geometric interface: the VLM predicts sparse 3D waypoints representing high-level intention, while GAE maps these waypoints together with real-time point cloud observations to continuous action trajectories. GAE is pretrained on a large-scale pointcloud-trajectory dataset comprising 150k trajectories from both simulation and real-world robots. To further improve efficiency and generalization, we introduce an Action Pre-training, Pointcloud Fine-tuning (APPF) scheme that decouples learning action dynamics from geometry grounding. After pretraining, GAE is frozen and reused across downstream tasks, requiring only lightweight fine-tuning of the VLM to produce the sparse interface. Experiments show that our method achieves strong performance and generalization across diverse visual domains, camera viewpoints, and natural language instructions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03896","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-12T02:19:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03992","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03992","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03992","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jehyeok","familyName":"Yeon","name":"Yeon, Jehyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isha","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Isha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gagandeep","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Gagandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantitative Certification of Agentic Tool Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T01:50:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-13T14:28:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-14T01:11:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in agentic systems, where a fundamental task is mapping user intents to relevant external tools. Errors in tool selection can have severe outcomes, such as unauthorized data access, even without modifying the agent's underlying model. Existing evaluations measure performance on curated, benign benchmarks. However, a pipeline's behavior in deployment depends on the tool pool the agent actually encounters, which in open registries is shaped by third parties. We introduce LLMCert-T, the first statistical framework that returns \\textbf{high-confidence upper bounds on the probability that a tool-selection pipeline satisfies a declared safety specification under a realistic tool distribution}. LLMCert-T models tool-selection evaluation as a Bernoulli estimation problem, drawing inserted-tool sequences from a distribution that the safety specification fixes. To evaluate robustness against realistic deployment conditions, we instantiate this distribution as a stochastic process that generates inserted-tool sequences round by round, conditioning each round on the agent's selection in the previous round. LLMCert-T aggregates the per-trial outcomes into a one-sided Clopper-Pearson upper bound on the probability that the specification is satisfied. By returning this bound as a certificate with statistical guarantees over the inserted-tool sequence distribution, LLMCert-T makes safety claims intuitive, actionable, and comparable across models, retrievers, mitigations, and registry policies. Across popular BFCL and OpenAPI tool pools, LLMCert-T shows that current LLM agents remain fragile under Distractor Selection and Top-N Saturation specifications: their certified correctness upper bounds drop to approximately 20\\%, far below their clean-pool lower bounds."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03992","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-14T02:50:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03895","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03895","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03895","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muzhi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Muzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Canyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Canyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongze","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Zongze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoman","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiduo","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Yiduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NoTVLA: Semantics-Preserving Robot Adaptation via Narrative Action Interfaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:26:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T17:14:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T02:30:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models represent a pivotal advance in embodied intelligence, yet they confront critical barriers to real-world deployment, most notably catastrophic forgetting. This issue stems from their overreliance on continuous action sequences or action chunks, which inadvertently create isolated data silos that disrupt knowledge retention across tasks. To tackle these challenges, we propose the Narrowing of Trajectory VLA (NoTVLA) framework: a novel approach that narrows its focus to sparse trajectories, thereby avoiding the catastrophic forgetting associated with dense trajectory fine-tuning. A key innovation of NoTVLA lies in its trajectory planning strategy: instead of centering on the target object's trajectory, it leverages temporal compression and spatial reasoning pruning specifically for the robot end effector's trajectory. Furthermore, training is conducted using these sparse trajectories rather than dense action trajectories, an optimization that delivers remarkable practical advantages with better performance in zero-shot. In multi-task evaluation scenarios, NoTVLA achieves superior performance and generalization compared to pi0 while operating under two critical constraints: it uses over an order of magnitude less computing power than pi0 and requires no wrist-mounted camera. This design ensures that NoTVLA's operational accuracy closely approximates that of single-task expert models. Crucially, it also preserves the model's inherent language capabilities, enabling zero-shot generalization in specific scenarios, supporting unified model deployment across multiple robot platforms, and fostering a degree of generalization even when perceiving tasks from novel perspectives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03895","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:03:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04008","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04008","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04008","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahil","familyName":"Joshi","name":"Joshi, Sahil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agniva","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Agniva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amar","familyName":"Kanakamedala","name":"Kanakamedala, Amar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ekam","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Ekam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anshumali","familyName":"Shrivastava","name":"Shrivastava, Anshumali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RACE Attention: A Strictly Linear-Time Attention Layer for Training on Outrageously Large Contexts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:57:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T01:09:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:15:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-15T18:01:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-17T02:03:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-01T16:35:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:18:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-18T14:33:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:45:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Softmax Attention has a quadratic time complexity in sequence length, which becomes prohibitive to run at long contexts, even with highly optimized GPU kernels. For example, FlashAttention-2/3 (exact, GPU-optimized implementations of Softmax Attention) cannot complete a single forward-backward pass of a single attention layer once the context exceeds ~4 million tokens on an NVIDIA GH200 (96 GB). We introduce Repeated Arrays-of-Count Estimators (RACE) Attention, a kernel-inspired alternative to Softmax Attention that is strictly linear in sequence length and embedding size. RACE Attention replaces the exponential kernel with a sharpened angular similarity, and approximates attention outputs via Gaussian random projections and soft Locality-Sensitive Hashing (LSH), avoiding construction of the full attention matrix. Across language modeling, masked language modeling, and text/image classification, RACE Attention matches or outperforms strong baselines up to 64K seqeuence length while reducing wall-clock time and memory usage. In addition, we conduct a controlled scaling study on a single attention layer and demonstrate processing of up to 12 million tokens on an NVIDIA GH200 GPU and 75 million tokens on an Intel Xeon Gold 5220R CPU in a single forward-backward pass, which is well beyond the capabilities of current state-of-the-art attention implementations. RACE Attention thus offers a practical and theoretically grounded mechanism for long-context training on today's hardware. We release our code at https://github.com/sahiljoshi515/RACE_Attention."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026. 29 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04008","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:56Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03923","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03923","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03923","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingsong","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Mingsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles","familyName":"Kulick","name":"Kulick, Charles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sui","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Sui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Convergence and Size Transferability of Continuous-depth Graph Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:59:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-18T00:12:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:23:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continuous-depth graph neural networks, also known as Graph Neural Differential Equations (GNDEs), combine the structural inductive bias of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) with the continuous-depth architecture of Neural ODEs, offering a scalable and principled framework for modeling dynamics on graphs. In this paper, we present a rigorous convergence analysis of GNDEs with time-varying parameters in the infinite-node limit, providing theoretical insights into their size transferability. To this end, we introduce Graphon Neural Differential Equations (Graphon-NDEs) as the infinite-node limit of GNDEs and establish their well-posedness. Leveraging tools from graphon theory and dynamical systems, we prove the trajectory-wise convergence of GNDE solutions to Graphon-NDE solutions. Moreover, we derive explicit convergence rates under two deterministic graph sampling regimes: (1) weighted graphs sampled from smooth graphons, and (2) unweighted graphs sampled from $\\{0,1\\}$-valued (discontinuous) graphons. We further establish size transferability bounds, providing theoretical justification for the practical strategy of transferring GNDE models trained on moderate-sized graphs to larger, structurally similar graphs without retraining. Numerical experiments using synthetic and real data support our theoretical findings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03923","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:51Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03988","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03988","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03988","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang Anh","familyName":"Just","name":"Just, Hoang Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myeongseob","familyName":"Ko","name":"Ko, Myeongseob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxi","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Ruoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Signal is in the Steps: Local Scoring for Reasoning Data Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T01:15:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:45:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-14T20:09:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:09:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Distilling long-form reasoning from teacher models into smaller students requires selecting which candidate solutions to train on. Recent work argues that one should select responses the student model assigns highest probability, i.e., favoring solutions ``natural'' to the student. However, we find that this approach works within a single teacher but fails when scaling to long reasoning traces from multiple diverse teachers. We identify a key cause: this approach scores entire solutions, but students generalize by recombining familiar reasoning steps, not by memorizing complete solutions. Full-trajectory scoring optimizes the wrong target; it rewards global fluency while the transferable signal lies in local step transitions. We propose Local Average Log Probability (LALP), which scores each reasoning step using only a small window of preceding context, measuring whether each step is justified by its immediate premises rather than whether the full response looks natural to the student. LALP enables two practical use cases: selecting the best teacher before fine-tuning and curating training data from diverse teacher pools. Across math, coding, and science reasoning tasks, LALP consistently improves accuracy when selecting the most natural solutions by a large margin."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03988","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03989","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03989","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03989","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue-Cheng","familyName":"Tai","name":"Tai, Xue-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raymond H.","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Raymond H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T01:16:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:45:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-12T14:04:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:05:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Transformer architecture has revolutionized the field of sequence modeling and underpins the recent breakthroughs in large language models (LLMs). However, a comprehensive mathematical theory that explains its structure and operations remains elusive. In this work, we propose a novel continuous framework that rigorously interprets the Transformer as a discretization of a structured integro-differential equation. Within this formulation, the self-attention mechanism emerges naturally as a non-local integral operator, and layer normalization is characterized as a projection to a time-dependent constraint. This operator-theoretic and variational perspective offers a unified and interpretable foundation for understanding the architecture's core components, including attention, feedforward layers, and normalization. Our approach extends beyond previous theoretical analyses by embedding the entire Transformer operation in continuous domains for both token indices and feature dimensions. This leads to a principled and flexible framework that not only deepens on theoretical insight but also offers new directions for architecture design, analysis, and control-based interpretations. This new interpretation provides a step toward bridging the gap between deep learning architectures and continuous mathematical modeling, and contributes a foundational perspective to the ongoing development of interpretable and theoretically grounded neural network models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03989","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:28Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03904","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03904","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03904","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangting","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Hangting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinmeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingchen","familyName":"Zhuge","name":"Zhuge, Mingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dandan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Dandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyuan","familyName":"Zha","name":"Zha, Hongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM as an Algorithmist: Enhancing Anomaly Detectors via Programmatic Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:00:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-30T05:47:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:21:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing anomaly detection (AD) methods for tabular data usually rely on some assumptions about anomaly patterns, leading to inconsistent performance in real-world scenarios. While Large Language Models (LLMs) show remarkable reasoning capabilities, their direct application to tabular AD is impeded by fundamental challenges, including difficulties in processing heterogeneous data and significant privacy risks. To address these limitations, we propose LLM-DAS, a novel framework that repositions the LLM from a ``data processor'' to an ``algorithmist''. Instead of being exposed to raw data, our framework leverages the LLM's ability to reason about algorithms. It analyzes a high-level description of a given detector to understand its intrinsic weaknesses and then generates detector-specific, data-agnostic Python code to synthesize ``hard-to-detect'' anomalies that exploit these vulnerabilities. This generated synthesis program, which is reusable across diverse datasets, is then instantiated to augment training data, systematically enhancing the detector's robustness by transforming the problem into a more discriminative two-class classification task. Extensive experiments on 36 TAD benchmarks show that LLM-DAS consistently boosts the performance of mainstream detectors. By bridging LLM reasoning with classic AD algorithms via programmatic synthesis, LLM-DAS offers a scalable, effective, and privacy-preserving approach to patching the logical blind spots of existing detectors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by the Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03904","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03929","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03929","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03929","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"De Bortoli","name":"De Bortoli, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnaud","familyName":"Doucet","name":"Doucet, Arnaud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Speculative Masked Diffusions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T20:16:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:42:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T10:45:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:38:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present self-speculative masked diffusions, a new class of masked diffusion generative models for discrete data that require significantly fewer function evaluations to generate samples. Standard masked diffusion models predict factorized logits over currently masked positions. A number of masked positions are then sampled, however, the factorization approximation means that sampling too many positions in one go leads to poor sample quality. As a result, many simulation steps and therefore neural network function evaluations are required to generate high-quality data. We reduce the computational burden by generating non-factorized predictions over masked positions. This is achieved by modifying the final transformer attention mask from non-causal to causal, enabling draft token generation and parallel validation via a novel, model-integrated speculative sampling mechanism. This results in a non-factorized predictive distribution over masked positions in a single forward pass. We apply our method to GPT2 scale text modelling and protein sequence generation, finding that we can achieve a ~2x reduction in the required number of network forward passes relative to standard masked diffusion models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 7 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03929","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:09:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03944","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03944","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03944","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Weiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqi","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Tianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijie","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Weijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T21:07:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:43:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-05T21:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:07:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) raise concerns about content authenticity and integrity because they can generate human-like text at scale. Text watermarks, which embed detectable statistical signals into generated text, offer a provable way to verify content origin. Many detection methods rely on pivotal statistics that are i.i.d. under human-written text, making goodness-of-fit (GoF) tests a natural tool for watermark detection. However, GoF tests remain largely underexplored in this setting. In this paper, we systematically evaluate eight GoF tests across three popular watermarking schemes, using three open-source LLMs, two datasets, various generation temperatures, and multiple post-editing methods. We find that general GoF tests can improve both the detection power and robustness of watermark detectors. Notably, we observe that text repetition, common in low-temperature settings, gives GoF tests a unique advantage not exploited by existing methods. Our results highlight that classic GoF tests are a simple yet powerful and underused tool for watermark detection in LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 as a spotlight"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03944","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-09T03:01:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Yeh","name":"Yeh, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jwala","familyName":"Dhamala","name":"Dhamala, Jwala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ousmane","familyName":"Dia","name":"Dia, Ousmane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LH-Deception: Simulating and Understanding LLM Deceptive Behaviors in Long-Horizon Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T15:30:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:58:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T12:03:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:43:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deception is a pervasive feature of human communication and an emerging concern in large language models (LLMs). While recent studies document instances of LLM deception, most evaluations remain confined to single-turn prompts and fail to capture the long-horizon interactions in which deceptive strategies typically unfold. We introduce a new simulation framework, LH-Deception, for a systematic, empirical quantification of deception in LLMs under extended sequences of interdependent tasks and dynamic contextual pressures. LH-Deception is designed as a multi-agent system: a performer agent tasked with completing tasks and a supervisor agent that evaluates progress, provides feedback, and maintains evolving states of trust. An independent deception auditor then reviews full trajectories to identify when and how deception occurs. We conduct extensive experiments across 11 frontier models, spanning both closed-source and open-source systems, and find that deception is model-dependent, increases with event pressure, and consistently erodes supervisor trust. Qualitative analyses further reveal emergent, long-horizon phenomena, such as ``chains of deception\", which are invisible to static, single-turn evaluations. Our findings provide a foundation for evaluating future LLMs in real-world, trust-sensitive contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:21:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03969","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03969","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03969","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengxiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chengxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isha","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Isha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weitong","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Weitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gagandeep","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Gagandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Catastrophic is Your LLM? Certifying Risk in Conversation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T23:00:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-04T16:24:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T02:00:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T02:22:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:17:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) can produce catastrophic responses in conversational settings that pose serious risks to public safety and security. Existing evaluations often fail to fully reveal these vulnerabilities because they rely on fixed attack prompt sequences, lack statistical guarantees, and do not scale to the vast space of multi-turn conversations. In this work, we propose C$^3$LLM, a novel, principled statistical Certification framework for Catastrophic risks in multi-turn Conversation for LLMs that bounds the probability of an LLM generating catastrophic responses under multi-turn conversation distributions with statistical guarantees. We model multi-turn conversations as probability distributions over query sequences, represented by a Markov process on a query graph whose edges encode semantic similarity to capture realistic conversational flow, and quantify catastrophic risks using confidence intervals. We define several inexpensive and practical distributions--random node, graph path, and adaptive with rejection. Our results demonstrate that these distributions can reveal substantial catastrophic risks in frontier models, with certified lower bounds as high as 70% for the worst model, highlighting the urgent need for improved safety training strategies in frontier LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03969","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:59Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:21:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03906","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03906","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03906","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ardalan","familyName":"Aryashad","name":"Aryashad, Ardalan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parsa","familyName":"Razmara","name":"Razmara, Parsa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Mahjoub","name":"Mahjoub, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyedarmin","familyName":"Azizi","name":"Azizi, Seyedarmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi","familyName":"Salmani","name":"Salmani, Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arad","familyName":"Firouzkouhi","name":"Firouzkouhi, Arad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Filters to VLMs: Benchmarking Defogging Methods through Object Detection and Segmentation Performance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:05:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T12:26:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:25:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous driving perception systems are particularly vulnerable in foggy conditions, where light scattering reduces contrast and obscures fine details critical for safe operation. While numerous defogging methods exist, from handcrafted filters to learned restoration models, improvements in image fidelity do not consistently translate into better downstream detection and segmentation. Moreover, prior evaluations often rely on synthetic data, raising concerns about real-world transferability.\n We present a structured empirical study that benchmarks a comprehensive set of defogging pipelines, including classical dehazing filters, modern defogging networks, chained variants combining filters and models, and prompt-driven visual language image editing models applied directly to foggy images. To bridge the gap between simulated and physical environments, we evaluate these pipelines on both the synthetic Foggy Cityscapes dataset and the real-world Adverse Conditions Dataset with Correspondences (ACDC).\n We examine generalization by evaluating performance on synthetic fog and real-world conditions, assessing both image quality and downstream perception in terms of object detection mean average precision and segmentation panoptic quality. Our analysis identifies when defogging is effective, the impact of combining models, and how visual language models compare to traditional approaches. We additionally report qualitative rubric-based evaluations from both human and visual language model judges and analyze their alignment with downstream task metrics.\n Together, these results establish a transparent, task-oriented benchmark for defogging methods and identify the conditions under which pre-processing meaningfully improves autonomous perception in adverse weather.\n Project page: https://aradfir.github.io/filters-to-vlms-defogging-page/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at WACV 2026 Proceedings (Oral), 5th Workshop on Image, Video, and Audio Quality Assessment in Computer Vision, with a focus on VLM and Diffusion Models"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03906","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:26Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:35:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04000","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04000","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04000","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rulong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rulong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangming","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Guangming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed Information Bottleneck Theory for Multi-Modal Task-Aware Semantic Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:18:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T05:59:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:53:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-31T15:34:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-01T02:07:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic communication shifts the focus from bit-level accuracy to task-relevant semantic delivery, enabling efficient and intelligent communication for next-generation networks. However, existing multi-modal solutions often process all available data modalities indiscriminately, ignoring that their contributions to downstream tasks are often unequal. This not only leads to severe resource inefficiency but also degrades task inference performance due to irrelevant or redundant information. To tackle this issue, we propose a novel task-aware distributed information bottleneck (TADIB) framework, which quantifies the contribution of any set of modalities to given tasks. Based on this theoretical framework, we design a practical coding scheme that intelligently selects and compresses only the most task-relevant modalities at the transmitter. To find the optimal selection and the codecs in the network, we adopt the probabilistic relaxation of discrete selection, enabling distributed encoders to make coordinated decisions with score function estimation and common randomness. Extensive experiments on public datasets demonstrate that our solution matches or surpasses the inference quality of full-modal baselines while significantly reducing communication and computational costs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04000","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-01T03:38:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03993","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03993","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03993","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxin","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhui","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Junhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Keep It on a Leash: Controllable Pseudo-label Generation Towards Realistic Long-Tailed Semi-Supervised Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T01:52:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T07:36:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:30:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:59:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:17:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-12T01:33:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:49:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-03T12:24:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:33:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-12-11T03:26:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-12-12T01:18:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current long-tailed semi-supervised learning methods assume that labeled data exhibit a long-tailed distribution, and unlabeled data adhere to a typical predefined distribution (i.e., long-tailed, uniform, or inverse long-tailed). However, the distribution of the unlabeled data is generally unknown and may follow an arbitrary distribution. To tackle this challenge, we propose a Controllable Pseudo-label Generation (CPG) framework, expanding the labeled dataset with the progressively identified reliable pseudo-labels from the unlabeled dataset and training the model on the updated labeled dataset with a known distribution, making it unaffected by the unlabeled data distribution. Specifically, CPG operates through a controllable self-reinforcing optimization cycle: (i) at each training step, our dynamic controllable filtering mechanism selectively incorporates reliable pseudo-labels from the unlabeled dataset into the labeled dataset, ensuring that the updated labeled dataset follows a known distribution; (ii) we then construct a Bayes-optimal classifier using logit adjustment based on the updated labeled data distribution; (iii) this improved classifier subsequently helps identify more reliable pseudo-labels in the next training step. We further theoretically prove that this optimization cycle can significantly reduce the generalization error under some conditions. Additionally, we propose a class-aware adaptive augmentation module to further improve the representation of minority classes, and an auxiliary branch to maximize data utilization by leveraging all labeled and unlabeled samples. Comprehensive evaluations on various commonly used benchmark datasets show that CPG achieves consistent improvements, surpassing state-of-the-art methods by up to $\\textbf{15.97%}$ in accuracy. The code is available at https://github.com/yaxinhou/CPG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The paper is accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03993","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:34Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-12T03:11:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03898","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03898","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03898","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nusrat Jahan","familyName":"Lia","name":"Lia, Nusrat Jahan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubhashis Roy","familyName":"Dipta","name":"Dipta, Shubhashis Roy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdullah Khan","familyName":"Zehady","name":"Zehady, Abdullah Khan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naymul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Naymul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madhusodan","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Madhusodan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdullah Al","familyName":"Wasif","name":"Wasif, Abdullah Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Read Between the Lines: A Benchmark for Uncovering Political Bias in Bangla News Articles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:34:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T18:39:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T03:07:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Detecting media bias is crucial, specifically in the South Asian region. Despite this, annotated datasets and computational studies for Bangla political bias research remain scarce. Crucially because, political stance detection in Bangla news requires understanding of linguistic cues, cultural context, subtle biases, rhetorical strategies, code-switching, implicit sentiment, and socio-political background. To address this, we introduce the first benchmark dataset of 200 politically significant and highly debated Bangla news articles, labeled for government-leaning, government-critique, and neutral stances, alongside diagnostic analyses for evaluating large language models (LLMs). Our comprehensive evaluation of 28 proprietary and open-source LLMs shows strong performance in detecting government-critique content (F1 up to 0.83) but substantial difficulty with neutral articles (F1 as low as 0.00). Models also tend to over-predict government-leaning stances, often misinterpreting ambiguous narratives. This dataset and its associated diagnostics provide a foundation for advancing stance detection in Bangla media research and offer insights for improving LLM performance in low-resource languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to BLP at AACL-IJCNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03898","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:14Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:15:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04022","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04022","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04022","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chendong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anlan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Anlan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lili","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Lili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suman","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Suman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video-in-the-Loop: Span-Grounded Long Video QA with Interleaved Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:03:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T17:55:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T04:05:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:22:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present \\emph{Video-in-the-Loop} (ViTL), a two-stage long-video QA framework that preserves a fixed token budget by first \\emph{localizing} question-relevant interval(s) with a low-fps skim and then \\emph{answering} via span-aware reallocation of visual tokens at higher effective frame rate, emitting an interleaved output with both spans and the final option for direct attribution. We also introduce \\dataname{}, which converts description based event graphs into \\emph{span-grounded} multiple-choice QA by pairing each question with \\emph{ground-truth} time span(s) and related reasoning. ViTL is trained end-to-end with an interleaved group-relative objective that couples temporal IoU for localization with answer correctness, allowing credit to flow from answers back to spans without increasing compute. Under fixed token budgets, ViTL attains up to 8.6% with 50% less frame input on long-video QA and temporal grounding (e.g., Charades-STA, ActivityNet-Captions) and ablations show that span-aware token reallocation consistently surpasses uniform sampling. Together, \\dataname{} and ViTL provide an interpretable, compute-efficient recipe for scalable long-video QA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04022","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:21:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04045","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04045","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04045","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yunfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathleen","familyName":"McKeown","name":"McKeown, Kathleen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Smaranda","familyName":"Muresan","name":"Muresan, Smaranda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Chain-of-Thought Reasoning for Steerable Pluralistic Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are typically trained to reflect a relatively uniform set of values, which limits their applicability to tasks that require understanding of nuanced human perspectives. Recent research has underscored the importance of enabling LLMs to support steerable pluralism -- the capacity to adopt a specific perspective and align generated outputs with it. In this work, we investigate whether Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning techniques can be applied to building steerable pluralistic models. We explore several methods, including CoT prompting, fine-tuning on human-authored CoT, fine-tuning on synthetic explanations, and Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR). We evaluate these approaches using the Value Kaleidoscope and OpinionQA datasets. Among the methods studied, RLVR consistently outperforms others and demonstrates strong training sample efficiency. We further analyze the generated CoT traces with respect to faithfulness and safety."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04045","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04044","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04044","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04044","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingtao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujia","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengzhi","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Mengzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongwei","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Hongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantization Range Estimation for Convolutional Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; K.3.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"00-01","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:35:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-training quantization for reducing the storage of deep neural network models has been demonstrated to be an effective way in various tasks. However, low-bit quantization while maintaining model accuracy is a challenging problem. In this paper, we present a range estimation method to improve the quantization performance for post-training quantization. We model the range estimation into an optimization problem of minimizing quantization errors by layer-wise local minima. We prove this problem is locally convex and present an efficient search algorithm to find the optimal solution. We propose the application of the above search algorithm to the transformed weights space to do further improvement in practice. Our experiments demonstrate that our method outperforms state-of-the-art performance generally on top-1 accuracy for image classification tasks on the ResNet series models and Inception-v3 model. The experimental results show that the proposed method has almost no loss of top-1 accuracy in 8-bit and 6-bit settings for image classifications, and the accuracy of 4-bit quantization is also significantly improved. The code is available at https://github.com/codeiscommitting/REQuant."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 5 tables, research report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04044","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04041","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04041","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04041","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayudh","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Ayudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Harsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep","familyName":"Routray","name":"Routray, Sandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi Rajesh","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Rishi Rajesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Esha","familyName":"Pahwa","name":"Pahwa, Esha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SITCOM: Scaling Inference-Time COMpute for VLAs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:24:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning robust robotic control policies remains a major challenge due to the high cost of collecting labeled data, limited generalization to unseen environments, and difficulties in planning over long horizons. While Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models offer a promising solution by grounding natural language instructions into single-step control commands, they often lack mechanisms for lookahead and struggle with compounding errors in dynamic tasks. In this project, we introduce Scaling Inference-Time COMpute for VLAs (SITCOM), a framework that augments any pretrained VLA with model-based rollouts and reward-based trajectory selection, inspired by Model Predictive Control algorithm. SITCOM leverages a learned dynamics model to simulate multi-step action rollouts to select the best candidate plan for real-world execution, transforming one-shot VLAs into robust long-horizon planners. We develop an efficient transformer-based dynamics model trained on large-scale BridgeV2 data and fine-tuned on SIMPLER environments to bridge the Real2Sim gap, and score candidate rollouts using rewards from simulator. Through comprehensive evaluation across multiple tasks and settings in the SIMPLER environment, we demonstrate that SITCOM when combined with a good reward function can significantly improve task completion rate from 48% to 72% using trained dynamics model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Space in Vision, Language, and Embodied AI (SpaVLE). *Equal contribution"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04041","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Nuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Payani","name":"Payani, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyi","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Mingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiwen","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Caiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"\\textsc{GUI-Spotlight}: Adaptive Iterative Focus Refinement for Enhanced GUI Visual Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:15:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have markedly expanded the competence of graphical user-interface (GUI) systems, propelling them beyond controlled simulations into complex, real-world environments across diverse platforms. However, practical usefulness is still bounded by the reliability of visual grounding, i.e., mapping textual references to exact on-screen elements. This limitation prevents the system from accurately performing pointer-level actions such as clicking or dragging. To address it, we introduce GUI-Spotlight -- a model trained for image-grounded reasoning that dynamically invokes multiple specialized tools to iteratively narrow its focus to the relevant region of the screen, thereby substantially improving visual grounding accuracy. On the ScreenSpot-Pro benchmark, GUI-Spotlight trained with only 18.5K training samples achieves 52.8\\% accuracy, surpassing V2P-7B (50.6\\% with 9.6M training samples) and GTA-1-7B (50.1\\% with 1.56M training samples)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04034","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04034","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04034","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linn","familyName":"Bieske","name":"Bieske, Linn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carla","familyName":"Lorente","name":"Lorente, Carla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt-to-Prompt: Text-Based Image Editing Via Cross-Attention Mechanisms -- The Research of Hyperparameters and Novel Mechanisms to Enhance Existing Frameworks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:56:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in image editing have shifted from manual pixel manipulation to employing deep learning methods like stable diffusion models, which now leverage cross-attention mechanisms for text-driven control. This transition has simplified the editing process but also introduced variability in results, such as inconsistent hair color changes. Our research aims to enhance the precision and reliability of prompt-to-prompt image editing frameworks by exploring and optimizing hyperparameters. We present a comprehensive study of the \"word swap\" method, develop an \"attention re-weight method\" for better adaptability, and propose the \"CL P2P\" framework to address existing limitations like cycle inconsistency. This work contributes to understanding and improving the interaction between hyperparameter settings and the architectural choices of neural network models, specifically their attention mechanisms, which significantly influence the composition and quality of the generated images."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04034","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:34Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04032","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04032","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04032","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Braden","familyName":"Teitge","name":"Teitge, Braden","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessalyn","familyName":"Holodinsky","name":"Holodinsky, Jessalyn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steve","familyName":"Drew","name":"Drew, Steve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Small Language Models for Emergency Departments Decision Support: A Benchmark Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:46:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have become increasingly popular in medical domains to assist physicians with a variety of clinical and operational tasks. Given the fast-paced and high-stakes environment of emergency departments (EDs), small language models (SLMs), characterized by a reduction in parameter count compared to LLMs, offer significant potential due to their inherent reasoning capability and efficient performance. This enables SLMs to support physicians by providing timely and accurate information synthesis, thereby improving clinical decision-making and workflow efficiency. In this paper, we present a comprehensive benchmark designed to identify SLMs suited for ED decision support, taking into account both specialized medical expertise and broad general problem-solving capabilities. In our evaluations, we focus on SLMs that have been trained on a mixture of general-domain and medical corpora. A key motivation for emphasizing SLMs is the practical hardware limitations, operational cost constraints, and privacy concerns in the typical real-world deployments. Our benchmark datasets include MedMCQA, MedQA-4Options, and PubMedQA, with the medical abstracts dataset emulating tasks aligned with real ED physicians' daily tasks. Experimental results reveal that general-domain SLMs surprisingly outperform their medically fine-tuned counterparts across these diverse benchmarks for ED. This indicates that for ED, specialized medical fine-tuning of the model may not be required."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to 2025 IEEE International Conference on Autonomous and Trusted Computing (ATC 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04032","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nelvin","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Nelvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James Asikin","familyName":"Cheung","name":"Cheung, James Asikin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Ching","familyName":"Shih","name":"Shih, Yu-Ching","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amol","familyName":"Salunkhe","name":"Salunkhe, Amol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:45:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are becoming useful in many domains due to their impressive abilities that arise from large training datasets and large model sizes. More recently, they have been shown to be very effective in textual classification tasks, motivating the need to explain the LLMs' decisions. Motivated by practical constrains where LLMs are black-boxed and LLM calls are expensive, we study how incorporating counterfactuals into LLM reasoning can affect the LLM's ability to identify the top words that have contributed to its classification decision. To this end, we introduce a framework called the decision changing rate that helps us quantify the importance of the top words in classification. Our experimental results show that using counterfactuals can be helpful."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhao","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xinhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengwei","familyName":"Teng","name":"Teng, Fengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Debate on RLVR Reasoning Capability Boundary: Shrinkage, Expansion, or Both? A Two-Stage Dynamic View"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:31:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ongoing debate on whether reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) expands or shrinks the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs) remains unresolved. Some studies contend that RLVR mainly improves sampling efficiency but at the expense of diversity and exploratory capacity, resulting in capability boundary shrinkage. In contrast, others demonstrate that prolonged training can lead to the emergence of novel reasoning strategies, suggesting capability boundary expansion. To reconcile these contradictory findings, we theoretically and empirically show that both perspectives are partially valid-each aligning with a separate phase in an inherent two-stage probability mass dynamic: (1) Exploitation stage: initially, the model primarily samples explored high-reward and low-reward tokens, while rarely selecting the potentially optimal token. Positive advantage estimates increase the probability of high-reward tokens and decrease those of low-reward tokens, yet the optimal token's probability remains largely unchanged during this stage. (2) Exploration stage: as training advances, the growth rate of previously acquired high-reward tokens slows as their probabilities approach saturation. When a potentially optimal token-now receiving positive advantage estimates-is occasionally sampled, its probability increases, while those of the originally high-reward tokens decrease. This dynamic suggests that over-exploitation during the exploitation stage may lead to capability boundary shrinkage, whereas prolonged training into the exploration stage can promote an expansion of the reasoning capability boundary. Building upon our insights, we revisit the potential of only using relative negative gradients for prolonging training, providing a theoretical and empirical foundation for the development of more advanced reasoning capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:25Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04027","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04027","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04027","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinseong","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jinseong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yujin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaewook","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaewook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:25:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the increasing need to safeguard data privacy in machine learning models, differential privacy (DP) is one of the major frameworks to build privacy-preserving models. Support Vector Machines (SVMs) are widely used traditional machine learning models due to their robust margin guarantees and strong empirical performance in binary classification. However, applying DP to multi-class SVMs is inadequate, as the standard one-versus-rest (OvR) and one-versus-one (OvO) approaches repeatedly query each data sample when building multiple binary classifiers, thus consuming the privacy budget proportionally to the number of classes. To overcome this limitation, we explore all-in-one SVM approaches for DP, which access each data sample only once to construct multi-class SVM boundaries with margin maximization properties. We propose a novel differentially Private Multi-class SVM (PMSVM) with weight and gradient perturbation methods, providing rigorous sensitivity and convergence analyses to ensure DP in all-in-one SVMs. Empirical results demonstrate that our approach surpasses existing DP-SVM methods in multi-class scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04027","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyan","familyName":"Bu","name":"Bu, Yuyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaofei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhui","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beizhe","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Beizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Fake News Video Detection via LLM-Driven Creative Process Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:05:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746252.3760933","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of fake news on short video platforms has become a new significant societal concern, necessitating automatic video-news-specific detection. Current detectors primarily rely on pattern-based features to separate fake news videos from real ones. However, limited and less diversified training data lead to biased patterns and hinder their performance. This weakness stems from the complex many-to-many relationships between video material segments and fabricated news events in real-world scenarios: a single video clip can be utilized in multiple ways to create different fake narratives, while a single fabricated event often combines multiple distinct video segments. However, existing datasets do not adequately reflect such relationships due to the difficulty of collecting and annotating large-scale real-world data, resulting in sparse coverage and non-comprehensive learning of the characteristics of potential fake news video creation. To address this issue, we propose a data augmentation framework, AgentAug, that generates diverse fake news videos by simulating typical creative processes. AgentAug implements multiple LLM-driven pipelines of four fabrication categories for news video creation, combined with an active learning strategy based on uncertainty sampling to select the potentially useful augmented samples during training. Experimental results on two benchmark datasets demonstrate that AgentAug consistently improves the performance of short video fake news detectors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACM CIKM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04023","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04023","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04023","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mizanur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Mizanur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amran","familyName":"Bhuiyan","name":"Bhuiyan, Amran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed Saidul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Mohammed Saidul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Tahmid Rahman","familyName":"Laskar","name":"Laskar, Md Tahmid Rahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ridwan","familyName":"Mahbub","name":"Mahbub, Ridwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Masry","name":"Masry, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shafiq","familyName":"Joty","name":"Joty, Shafiq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enamul","familyName":"Hoque","name":"Hoque, Enamul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Based Data Science Agents: A Survey of Capabilities, Challenges, and Future Directions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled a new class of AI agents that automate multiple stages of the data science workflow by integrating planning, tool use, and multimodal reasoning across text, code, tables, and visuals. This survey presents the first comprehensive, lifecycle-aligned taxonomy of data science agents, systematically analyzing and mapping forty-five systems onto the six stages of the end-to-end data science process: business understanding and data acquisition, exploratory analysis and visualization, feature engineering, model building and selection, interpretation and explanation, and deployment and monitoring. In addition to lifecycle coverage, we annotate each agent along five cross-cutting design dimensions: reasoning and planning style, modality integration, tool orchestration depth, learning and alignment methods, and trust, safety, and governance mechanisms. Beyond classification, we provide a critical synthesis of agent capabilities, highlight strengths and limitations at each stage, and review emerging benchmarks and evaluation practices. Our analysis identifies three key trends: most systems emphasize exploratory analysis, visualization, and modeling while neglecting business understanding, deployment, and monitoring; multimodal reasoning and tool orchestration remain unresolved challenges; and over 90% lack explicit trust and safety mechanisms. We conclude by outlining open challenges in alignment stability, explainability, governance, and robust evaluation frameworks, and propose future research directions to guide the development of robust, trustworthy, low-latency, transparent, and broadly accessible data science agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Survey paper; 45 data science agents; under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04023","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04016","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04016","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04016","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanapol","familyName":"Popit","name":"Popit, Thanapol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natthapath","familyName":"Rungseesiripak","name":"Rungseesiripak, Natthapath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monthol","familyName":"Charattrakool","name":"Charattrakool, Monthol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saksorn","familyName":"Ruangtanusak","name":"Ruangtanusak, Saksorn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thai Semantic End-of-Turn Detection for Real-Time Voice Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:31:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fluid voice-to-voice interaction requires reliable and low-latency detection of when a user has finished speaking. Traditional audio-silence end-pointers add hundreds of milliseconds of delay and fail under hesitations or language-specific phenomena. We present, to our knowledge, the first systematic study of Thai text-only end-of-turn (EOT) detection for real-time agents. We compare zero-shot and few-shot prompting of compact LLMs to supervised fine-tuning of lightweight transformers. Using transcribed subtitles from the YODAS corpus and Thai-specific linguistic cues (e.g., sentence-final particles), we formulate EOT as a binary decision over token boundaries. We report a clear accuracy-latency tradeoff and provide a public-ready implementation plan. This work establishes a Thai baseline and demonstrates that small, fine-tuned models can deliver near-instant EOT decisions suitable for on-device agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IEEE ICSEC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04016","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04013","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04013","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04013","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jivitesh","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Jivitesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mona","familyName":"Diab","name":"Diab, Mona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishant","familyName":"Subramani","name":"Subramani, Nishant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Microscope: What Model Internals Reveal About Answer Correctness and Context Utilization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:14:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although large language models (LLMs) have tremendous utility, trustworthiness is still a chief concern: models often generate incorrect information with high confidence. While contextual information can help guide generation, identifying when a query would benefit from retrieved context and assessing the effectiveness of that context remains challenging. In this work, we operationalize interpretability methods to ascertain whether we can predict the correctness of model outputs from the model's activations alone. We also explore whether model internals contain signals about the efficacy of external context. We consider correct, incorrect, and irrelevant context and introduce metrics to distinguish amongst them. Experiments on six different models reveal that a simple classifier trained on intermediate layer activations of the first output token can predict output correctness with about 75% accuracy, enabling early auditing. Our model-internals-based metric significantly outperforms prompting baselines at distinguishing between correct and incorrect context, guarding against inaccuracies introduced by polluted context. These findings offer a lens to better understand the underlying decision-making processes of LLMs. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/jiarui-liu/LLM-Microscope"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04013","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04009","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04009","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04009","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zicong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boxuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Boxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixiang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Ruixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Shapes a Creative Machine Mind? Comprehensively Benchmarking Creativity in Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:00:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The meteoric rise of foundation models (FMs) has expanded their capabilities far beyond conventional tasks. Creativity, long regarded as a hallmark of human intelligence and a driver of innovation, is now increasingly recognized as a critical dimension of machine intelligence in the era of generative FMs, complementing traditional measures of accuracy. However, existing evaluation frameworks for creativity remain fragmented, relying on ad hoc metrics not firmly grounded in established theories. To address this gap, we introduce C^2-Eval, a holistic benchmark for unified assessment of creativity in FMs. C^2-Eval distinguishes between two complementary forms of creativity: convergent creativity, where tasks admit constrained solutions (e.g., code generation), and divergent creativity, where tasks are open-ended (e.g., storytelling). It evaluates both dimensions using fine-grained criteria derived from social-science theory, focusing on Usefulness, Originality, and Surprise (U-O-S). Through extensive experiments on leading proprietary and open-source models, we analyze trade-offs in their creative capabilities. Our results highlight both the strengths and challenges of current FMs in pursuing a creative machine mind, showing that C^2-Eval is an effective lens for examining the evolving landscape of creative AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04009","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03987","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03987","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03987","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ICEPool: Enhancing Graph Pooling Networks with Inter-cluster Connectivity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T01:13:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:45:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hierarchical Pooling Models have demonstrated strong performance in classifying graph-structured data. While numerous innovative methods have been proposed to design cluster assignments and coarsening strategies, the relationships between clusters are often overlooked. In this paper, we introduce Inter-cluster Connectivity Enhancement Pooling (ICEPool), a novel hierarchical pooling framework designed to enhance model's understanding of inter-cluster connectivity and ability of preserving the structural integrity in the original graph. ICEPool is compatible with a wide range of pooling-based GNN models. The deployment of ICEPool as an enhancement to existing models effectively combines the strengths of the original model with ICEPool's capability to emphasize the integration of inter-cluster connectivity, resulting in a more comprehensive and robust graph-level representation. Moreover, we make theoretical analysis to ICEPool's ability of graph reconstruction to demonstrate its effectiveness in learning inter-cluster relationship that is overlooked by conventional models. Finally, the experimental results show the compatibility of ICEPool with wide varieties of models and its potential to boost the performance of existing graph neural network architectures."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03987","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:25Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03984","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03984","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03984","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirandeep","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Kirandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Preetam Prabhu Srikar","familyName":"Dammu","name":"Dammu, Preetam Prabhu Srikar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hideo","familyName":"Joho","name":"Joho, Hideo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chirag","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Chirag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T00:35:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767695.3769484","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Personalized AI agents are becoming central to modern information retrieval, yet most evaluation methodologies remain static, relying on fixed benchmarks and one-off metrics that fail to reflect how users' needs evolve over time. These limitations hinder our ability to assess whether agents can meaningfully adapt to individuals across dynamic, longitudinal interactions. In this perspective paper, we propose a conceptual lens for rethinking evaluation in adaptive personalization, shifting the focus from static performance snapshots to interaction-aware, evolving assessments. We organize this lens around three core components: (1) persona-based user simulation with temporally evolving preference models; (2) structured elicitation protocols inspired by reference interviews to extract preferences in context; and (3) adaptation-aware evaluation mechanisms that measure how agent behavior improves across sessions and tasks. While recent works have embraced LLM-driven user simulation, we situate this practice within a broader paradigm for evaluating agents over time. To illustrate our ideas, we conduct a case study in e-commerce search using the PersonalWAB dataset. Beyond presenting a framework, our work lays a conceptual foundation for understanding and evaluating personalization as a continuous, user-centric endeavor."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03984","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03979","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03979","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03979","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emerson","familyName":"Melo","name":"Melo, Emerson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Müller","name":"Müller, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Softmax: A New Perspective on Gradient Bandits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T23:43:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We establish a link between a class of discrete choice models and the theory of online learning and multi-armed bandits. Our contributions are: (i) sublinear regret bounds for a broad algorithmic family, encompassing Exp3 as a special case; (ii) a new class of adversarial bandit algorithms derived from generalized nested logit models \\citep{wen:2001}; and (iii) \\textcolor{black}{we introduce a novel class of generalized gradient bandit algorithms that extends beyond the widely used softmax formulation. By relaxing the restrictive independence assumptions inherent in softmax, our framework accommodates correlated learning dynamics across actions, thereby broadening the applicability of gradient bandit methods.} Overall, the proposed algorithms combine flexible model specification with computational efficiency via closed-form sampling probabilities. Numerical experiments in stochastic bandit settings demonstrate their practical effectiveness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03979","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:14Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03978","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03978","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03978","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min Woo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Min Woo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Lozano","name":"Lozano, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier Gamazo","familyName":"Tejero","name":"Tejero, Javier Gamazo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishwesh","familyName":"Nath","name":"Nath, Vishwesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao Xiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiao Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Burgess","name":"Burgess, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Tibshirani","name":"Tibshirani, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Huver","name":"Huver, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serena","familyName":"Yeung-Levy","name":"Yeung-Levy, Serena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"No Tokens Wasted: Leveraging Long Context in Biomedical Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T23:38:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Embedding vision-language models (VLMs) are typically pretrained with short text windows (\u0026lt;77 tokens), which forces the truncation of long-format captions. Yet, the distribution of biomedical captions from large-scale open source literature reveals that a huge portion of captions far exceed 77 tokens. To this end, we investigate the impact of pretraining on long-format biomedical captions by extending the context length of text encoders in VLMs. We find that longer context (thus, enabling additional supervision provided in long-format captions) correlates with better retrieval and classification performance. Given this finding, we introduce BIOMEDICA-LongCAP, a dataset of 1M image-caption pairs enriched with context-aware descriptions from full-text articles, providing longer and additional textual supervision. Using BIOMEDICA-LongCAP, we train BMC-LongCLIP, a long-context biomedical VLM with a text encoder supporting windows of up to 512 tokens. Our model extends context capacity by 6.6x, reducing token waste from 55% to just 2.2%. On long-caption retrieval benchmarks, BMC-LongCLIP achieves up to +30% absolute gains in Recall@1 and +2% average improvements in classification, while also converging faster than short-context. Our results demonstrate that long-context modeling is a promising direction for advancing biomedical VLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03978","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03971","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03971","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03971","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jatin","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Jatin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirudh","familyName":"Buvanesh","name":"Buvanesh, Anirudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T23:10:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) with outcome-based rewards has proven effective for improving large language models (LLMs) on complex reasoning tasks. However, its success often depends on the base model occasionally sampling correct solutions. When no correct solutions are sampled, training encounters a zero-reward barrier where learning stalls due to zero gradients. We study this scenario through the graph search task introduced in Bachmann et al. (2024) and evaluate recent methods that incorporate desirable components such as dense rewards, diversity incentives, and improved credit assignment. Our experiments show that none of these approaches overcome the zero-reward barrier if the base model never produces a correct answer. In contrast, we find that a simple data-centric intervention of adding easier samples to the training set enables the model to eventually solve the original hard task despite starting from zero reward. Importantly, this succeeds without modifying the RL algorithm itself. Because official implementations of several baselines were unavailable, we developed our own, which allowed us to conduct a detailed analysis of their failure modes. We release these implementations to support further research at: https://github.com/rl4reasoning/rl-baselines"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03971","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03955","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03955","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03955","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sameep","familyName":"Vani","name":"Vani, Sameep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreyas","familyName":"Jena","name":"Jena, Shreyas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maitreya","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Maitreya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chitta","familyName":"Baral","name":"Baral, Chitta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somak","familyName":"Aditya","name":"Aditya, Somak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yezhou","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yezhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Harnessing Synthetic Preference Data for Enhancing Temporal Understanding of Video-LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T21:48:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:43:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Video Large Language Models (Video-LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable performance across general video understanding benchmarks-particularly in video captioning and descriptive tasks-they consistently underperform on tasks that require fine-grained temporal understanding. This limitation arises due to the lack of visual complexity and temporal nuance in current fine-tuning datasets, leading these models to rely heavily on language-based reasoning rather than truly understanding video dynamics. In this work, we propose TimeWarp, a systematic method to create a targeted synthetic temporal dataset to fine-tune the model's responses to encourage it to focus on the given input video. We introduce a large-scale preference dataset, created using TimeWarp, that captures intricate temporal dynamics often overlooked, grounding the model's responses to visual and temporal information. We demonstrate that when our method is applied to existing models, it significantly improves performance on temporal understanding benchmarks, highlighting the effectiveness of our proposed datasets in advancing temporal understanding in Video-LLMs, resulting in an absolute improvement in performance across seven benchmarks. Code is available at https://github.com/sameepv21/timewarp."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 9 figures, 6 tables. Presents TimeWarp, a synthetic preference data framework to improve temporal understanding in Video-LLMs, showing consistent gains across seven benchmarks. Includes supplementary material in the Appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03955","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:02:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03930","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03930","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03930","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huascar","familyName":"Sanchez","name":"Sanchez, Huascar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Briland","familyName":"Hitaj","name":"Hitaj, Briland","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T20:21:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:42:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-LLM collaboration promises accurate, robust, and context-aware solutions, yet existing approaches rely on implicit selection and output assessment without analyzing whether collaborating models truly complement or conflict. We introduce LLM Chemistry -- a framework that measures when LLM combinations exhibit synergistic or antagonistic behaviors that shape collective performance beyond individual capabilities. We formalize the notion of chemistry among LLMs, propose algorithms that quantify it by analyzing interaction dependencies, and recommend optimal model ensembles accordingly. Our theoretical analysis shows that chemistry among collaborating LLMs is most evident under heterogeneous model profiles, with its outcome impact shaped by task type, group size, and complexity. Evaluation on classification, summarization, and program repair tasks provides initial evidence for these task-dependent effects, thereby reinforcing our theoretical results. This establishes LLM Chemistry as both a diagnostic factor in multi-LLM systems and a foundation for ensemble recommendation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 5 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03930","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:02:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03926","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03926","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03926","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Kopte","name":"Kopte, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Kaup","name":"Kaup, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sliding Window Attention for Learned Video Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T20:11:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To manage the complexity of transformers in video compression, local attention mechanisms are a practical necessity. The common approach of partitioning frames into patches, however, creates architectural flaws like irregular receptive fields. When adapted for temporal autoregressive models, this paradigm, exemplified by the Video Compression Transformer (VCT), also necessitates computationally redundant overlapping windows. This work introduces 3D Sliding Window Attention (SWA), a patchless form of local attention. By enabling a decoder-only architecture that unifies spatial and temporal context processing, and by providing a uniform receptive field, our method significantly improves rate-distortion performance, achieving Bjørntegaard Delta-rate savings of up to 18.6 % against the VCT baseline. Simultaneously, by eliminating the need for overlapping windows, our method reduces overall decoder complexity by a factor of 2.8, while its entropy model is nearly 3.5 times more efficient. We further analyze our model's behavior and show that while it benefits from long-range temporal context, excessive context can degrade performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for PCS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03926","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03917","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03917","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03917","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinod","familyName":"Raman","name":"Raman, Vinod","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenghao","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Shenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samson","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Samson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transductive and Learning-Augmented Online Regression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:41:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Motivated by the predictable nature of real-life in data streams, we study online regression when the learner has access to predictions about future examples. In the extreme case, called transductive online learning, the sequence of examples is revealed to the learner before the game begins. For this setting, we fully characterize the minimax expected regret in terms of the fat-shattering dimension, establishing a separation between transductive online regression and (adversarial) online regression. Then, we generalize this setting by allowing for noisy or \\emph{imperfect} predictions about future examples. Using our results for the transductive online setting, we develop an online learner whose minimax expected regret matches the worst-case regret, improves smoothly with prediction quality, and significantly outperforms the worst-case regret when future example predictions are precise, achieving performance similar to the transductive online learner. This enables learnability for previously unlearnable classes under predictable examples, aligning with the broader learning-augmented model paradigm."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03917","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03915","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03915","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03915","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagar","familyName":"Bharadwaj","name":"Bharadwaj, Sagar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harrison","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, Harrison","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srinivasan","familyName":"Seshan","name":"Seshan, Srinivasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony","familyName":"Rowe","name":"Rowe, Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OpenFLAME: Federated Visual Positioning System to Enable Large-Scale Augmented Reality Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:41:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"World-scale augmented reality (AR) applications need a ubiquitous 6DoF localization backend to anchor content to the real world consistently across devices. Large organizations such as Google and Niantic are 3D scanning outdoor public spaces in order to build their own Visual Positioning Systems (VPS). These centralized VPS solutions fail to meet the needs of many future AR applications -- they do not cover private indoor spaces because of privacy concerns, regulations, and the labor bottleneck of updating and maintaining 3D scans. In this paper, we present OpenFLAME, a federated VPS backend that allows independent organizations to 3D scan and maintain a separate VPS service for their own spaces. This enables access control of indoor 3D scans, distributed maintenance of the VPS backend, and encourages larger coverage. Sharding of VPS services introduces several unique challenges -- coherency of localization results across spaces, quality control of VPS services, selection of the right VPS service for a location, and many others. We introduce the concept of federated image-based localization and provide reference solutions for managing and merging data across maps without sharing private data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03915","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03912","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03912","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03912","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyuan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Liyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jitao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenke","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhenke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengchun","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Chengchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalized Fitted Q-Iteration with Clustered Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:33:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper focuses on reinforcement learning (RL) with clustered data, which is commonly encountered in healthcare applications. We propose a generalized fitted Q-iteration (FQI) algorithm that incorporates generalized estimating equations into policy learning to handle the intra-cluster correlations. Theoretically, we demonstrate (i) the optimalities of our Q-function and policy estimators when the correlation structure is correctly specified, and (ii) their consistencies when the structure is mis-specified. Empirically, through simulations and analyses of a mobile health dataset, we find the proposed generalized FQI achieves, on average, a half reduction in regret compared to the standard FQI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03912","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03910","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03910","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03910","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akhil","familyName":"Padmanabha","name":"Padmanabha, Akhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessie","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Jessie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanisha","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Tanisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajat Kumar","familyName":"Jenamani","name":"Jenamani, Rajat Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victoria","familyName":"de León","name":"de León, Victoria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony","familyName":"Wertz","name":"Wertz, Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Janavi","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Janavi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Dodson","name":"Dodson, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunting","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yunting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carmel","familyName":"Majidi","name":"Majidi, Carmel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tapomayukh","familyName":"Bhattacharjee","name":"Bhattacharjee, Tapomayukh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zackory","familyName":"Erickson","name":"Erickson, Zackory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WAFFLE: A Wearable Approach to Bite Timing Estimation in Robot-Assisted Feeding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:19:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Millions of people around the world need assistance with feeding. Robotic feeding systems offer the potential to enhance autonomy and quality of life for individuals with impairments and reduce caregiver workload. However, their widespread adoption has been limited by technical challenges such as estimating bite timing, the appropriate moment for the robot to transfer food to a user's mouth. In this work, we introduce WAFFLE: Wearable Approach For Feeding with LEarned bite timing, a system that accurately predicts bite timing by leveraging wearable sensor data to be highly reactive to natural user cues such as head movements, chewing, and talking. We train a supervised regression model on bite timing data from 14 participants and incorporate a user-adjustable assertiveness threshold to convert predictions into proceed or stop commands. In a study with 15 participants without motor impairments with the Obi feeding robot, WAFFLE performs statistically on par with or better than baseline methods across measures of feeling of control, robot understanding, and workload, and is preferred by the majority of participants for both individual and social dining. We further demonstrate WAFFLE's generalizability in a study with 2 participants with motor impairments in their home environments using a Kinova 7DOF robot. Our findings support WAFFLE's effectiveness in enabling natural, reactive bite timing that generalizes across users, robot hardware, robot positioning, feeding trajectories, foods, and both individual and social dining contexts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03910","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03909","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03909","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03909","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyelin","familyName":"Nam","name":"Nam, Hyelin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyojun","familyName":"Go","name":"Go, Hyojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byeongjun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Byeongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byung-Hoon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Byung-Hoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyungjin","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Hyungjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generating Human Motion Videos using a Cascaded Text-to-Video Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:16:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human video generation is becoming an increasingly important task with broad applications in graphics, entertainment, and embodied AI. Despite the rapid progress of video diffusion models (VDMs), their use for general-purpose human video generation remains underexplored, with most works constrained to image-to-video setups or narrow domains like dance videos. In this work, we propose CAMEO, a cascaded framework for general human motion video generation. It seamlessly bridges Text-to-Motion (T2M) models and conditional VDMs, mitigating suboptimal factors that may arise in this process across both training and inference through carefully designed components. Specifically, we analyze and prepare both textual prompts and visual conditions to effectively train the VDM, ensuring robust alignment between motion descriptions, conditioning signals, and the generated videos. Furthermore, we introduce a camera-aware conditioning module that connects the two stages, automatically selecting viewpoints aligned with the input text to enhance coherence and reduce manual intervention. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach on both the MovieGen benchmark and a newly introduced benchmark tailored to the T2M-VDM combination, while highlighting its versatility across diverse use cases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 7 figures, Project Page:https://hyelinnam.github.io/Cameo/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03909","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03903","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03903","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03903","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md.","familyName":"Atabuzzaman","name":"Atabuzzaman, Md.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zero-Shot Fine-Grained Image Classification Using Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:56:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have demonstrated impressive performance on vision-language reasoning tasks. However, their potential for zero-shot fine-grained image classification, a challenging task requiring precise differentiation between visually similar categories, remains underexplored. We present a novel method that transforms zero-shot fine-grained image classification into a visual question-answering framework, leveraging LVLMs' comprehensive understanding capabilities rather than relying on direct class name generation. We enhance model performance through a novel attention intervention technique. We also address a key limitation in existing datasets by developing more comprehensive and precise class description benchmarks. We validate the effectiveness of our method through extensive experimentation across multiple fine-grained image classification benchmarks. Our proposed method consistently outperforms the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) approach, demonstrating both the effectiveness of our method and the broader potential of LVLMs for zero-shot fine-grained classification tasks. Code and Datasets: https://github.com/Atabuzzaman/Fine-grained-classification"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03903","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03893","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03893","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03893","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshay","familyName":"Kudva","name":"Kudva, Akshay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joel A.","familyName":"Paulson","name":"Paulson, Joel A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BONSAI: Structure-exploiting robust Bayesian optimization for networked black-box systems under uncertainty"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:16:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.compchemeng.2025.109393","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optimal design under uncertainty remains a fundamental challenge in advancing reliable, next-generation process systems. Robust optimization (RO) offers a principled approach by safeguarding against worst-case scenarios across a range of uncertain parameters. However, traditional RO methods typically require known problem structure, which limits their applicability to high-fidelity simulation environments. To overcome these limitations, recent work has explored robust Bayesian optimization (RBO) as a flexible alternative that can accommodate expensive, black-box objectives. Existing RBO methods, however, generally ignore available structural information and struggle to scale to high-dimensional settings. In this work, we introduce BONSAI (Bayesian Optimization of Network Systems under uncertAInty), a new RBO framework that leverages partial structural knowledge commonly available in simulation-based models. Instead of treating the objective as a monolithic black box, BONSAI represents it as a directed graph of interconnected white- and black-box components, allowing the algorithm to utilize intermediate information within the optimization process. We further propose a scalable Thompson sampling-based acquisition function tailored to the structured RO setting, which can be efficiently optimized using gradient-based methods. We evaluate BONSAI across a diverse set of synthetic and real-world case studies, including applications in process systems engineering. Compared to existing simulation-based RO algorithms, BONSAI consistently delivers more sample-efficient and higher-quality robust solutions, highlighting its practical advantages for uncertainty-aware design in complex engineering systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Computers and Chemical Engineering, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03893","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03891","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03891","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03891","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shawn Shuoshuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shawn Shuoshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiyaan","familyName":"Arfeen","name":"Arfeen, Daiyaan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minlan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Minlan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Steenkiste","name":"Steenkiste, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srinivasan","familyName":"Seshan","name":"Seshan, Srinivasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Co-adapting Machine Learning Job Shape and Cluster Topology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:05:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Allocating resources to distributed machine learning jobs in multi-tenant torus-topology clusters must meet each job's specific placement and communication requirements, which are typically described using shapes. There is an inherent tension between minimizing network contention and maximizing cluster utilization when placing various-shaped jobs. While existing schedulers typically optimize for one objective at the expense of the other, we demonstrate that both can be achieved simultaneously.\n Our proposed approach, RFold, adapts both job shapes and the underlying cluster topology at runtime. This is accomplished by combining two techniques: (1) identifying homomorphic job shapes that support the jobs communication needs, and (2) reconfiguring the optical circuit switch-enabled topology to support more diverse job shapes. Preliminary evaluation performed on a 4096-node torus cluster simulator indicates that RFold can improve absolute cluster utilization by 57% and reduce job completion time by up to 11x relative to existing methods"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03891","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":50,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"ht